Include the Mamba/GDN state layout in the KV offload cache identity.

The offload cache's directory name is a sha256 over FileMapper.fields, and
that set contained nothing about the recurrent state:

  blocks_per_file, dcp_size, dtype, inference_engine, model_name,
  kv_cache_groups[{layer_names, tokens_per_block}], parallel_agnostic,
  pcp_size, pp_size, tokens_per_hash, tp_size

`dtype` there is the ATTENTION KV dtype (fp8 here), not the SSM state dtype.
So two servers differing only in --mamba-ssm-cache-dtype (or in
--mamba-cache-mode) hash to the SAME directory, and each will happily load
the other's blocks and reinterpret the bytes. That is not a cache miss that
costs a recompute -- it is silent state corruption, and it comes out as
fluent-looking multilingual token soup or a run of "!!!!!!" from the first
decoded token, with the request otherwise looking perfectly healthy (high
cache hit rate, normal timings, no warning anywhere).

This model has 4 KV groups sharing that one directory -- 3 MambaManager
(48 linear_attn layers total) and 1 FullAttentionManager -- so on a resume
three quarters of the restored blocks are recurrent state.

Diagnosed 2026-08-24. One cache directory here had accumulated blocks from
fp16 / bf16 / fp32 runs across several days and several vLLM builds. With a
2-conversation x 88K-token repro, the conversation that got evicted and
restored from that directory was garbage 4/4, while the one that stayed
GPU-resident was always fine. Controls, all with the same full restores
("GPU had 0 tok"):

  offload disabled                          0/8  garbage (recompute instead)
  RAM tier only, 5 full restores            0/8
  NVMe tier, cache cleared first            0/8, 0/16, 0/6
  NVMe tier, pre-existing mixed-config dir  4/4, 15/24, 18/24

i.e. corruption occurred iff the mixed-config directory was in play, at both
--long-prefill-token-threshold 512 and 832, with O_DIRECT and with buffered
I/O. The threshold correlation that first pointed here was exposure, not
cause: 832 drove 433 offload lookups in one window against 57 at 512, so it
met a poisoned block far more often.

The two fields are written only when non-empty, following the existing
`replicated_layout` convention directly above, and they are populated only
when the model actually has a non-AttentionSpec group. Models with no
recurrent groups therefore keep their exact current directory hash and do
not lose their cache to this change. Verified: fp16 and fp32 now hash to
different directories, cache_mode likewise, the same config is stable across
runs, and a model with no mamba groups is byte-identical to before.

For a model that does have them this is a one-time cold rebuild -- the old
directory is simply orphaned and becomes the least-recently-accessed thing
in root_dir, so patches/fs-tier-disk-cap.patch reclaims it on its own.

Deliberately NOT hashed: the vLLM build/patch set. It would be stricter, but
it would cost a full cold cache on every patch applied to this repo, which
is a bad trade for a cache that takes ~65s per 88K-token conversation to
rebuild. The fields here are the ones that change what the stored bytes MEAN.

